-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1045 "Invertible Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7d6120fd-aa08-5562-9120-ce173e8fe311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125021 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3cbdac583a4cdf1b5eb761892934516a0b4edd1487bd402ae0014859c4a0ad53.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in (-2,2]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125021 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/d7/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/ce/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1061 13908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b61f81e-5366-54d7-b4dc-eb12246fa126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1061 13909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "619ae142-fadd-53d9-975f-a485d1f363b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1061 13911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "067f6188-2d0b-5a28-8513-b138cb5eda82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1061 13916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "956e822f-5a25-5d3b-ac84-ada9c2c8dee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1064 "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1065 2768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90b8b9a7-2b60-5a13-9829-c02575ad8c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321753 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321753 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321753 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4caea11f-484f-50a4-a520-52df284329ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321816 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321816 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321816 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "54e75cb9-e5b0-5ea8-9eaf-304e5de715df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322081 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322081 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322081 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7739ff43-c68c-58ca-8bbe-3cf898ab85f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322236 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322236 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322236 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322273 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322273 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322273 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d033687-0dda-5d24-a50d-81609e131df6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322283 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322283 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f052679-93df-563a-8c3a-49901beaf19d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322661 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322661 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322661 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c304d552-6f05-5067-a107-0c7b855a1c17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322736 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322736 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322736 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc2a112f-a59c-5195-8734-7545ee263058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323031 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323031 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323031 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323112 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323112 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323112 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f99f1c2f-bc18-5796-a3e2-28f9f9b5b60a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186323031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323373 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "661e0d23-dac0-58fe-aeb6-82bf0e4b60b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323456 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9392c5de-5972-51d1-8356-6d7fe3ba1f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323737 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in [-6,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [-6,4)$\n\nIII. $x\\in [4,10)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "544516cf-cdec-5de8-af07-1ff7e5be101e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323737 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/7c/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/00/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334106 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334106 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334106 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d39eb8e-2acf-5989-93c9-5706e83e7f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334184 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86eabbba-0a20-58a9-b168-1143669ceead" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334948 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334948 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334948 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1269f37-8751-57fd-a7c9-4c6a8b91e67d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334972 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334972 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334972 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335003 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e693a8f1-2c29-5ee8-9bb7-6a65568289ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335539 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a0d2f2b9-b380-5289-b8eb-9732e9ebb27b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335903 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335903 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335903 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335903 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=-x+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02e022db-b323-5e31-af64-39f79b6a6465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335903 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/aa/aaa05d1ab0fd630dacd81723fe00472cb901fb6f73af5f111f44459476191c0f.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
